<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sharif-of-mecca">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sharif of Mecca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sharifate of Mecca, Emir of Mecca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sharif of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> was the ruler of Mecca and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, chosen from among the descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> through his grandson Hasan, from the 10th century until 1925. The sharifs usually acknowledged the overlordship of a stronger Muslim power, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and their recognition lent prestige to claimants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. The last sharifs, of the Hashemite family, lost the Hejaz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca.jpg" alt="Sharif of Mecca" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mrmw PaFra · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scherifen_von_Mekka_Stammtafel_I.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word sharif is Arabic for noble or highborn and describes the descendants of Hashim ibn Abd Manaf; the title is sometimes written Sheriff or Sherif, the form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a> used in Seven Pillars of Wisdom. The office dates from the late Abbasid era. Its holder was the traditional steward of the holy cities, charged with protecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and its surroundings and with the safety of the pilgrims performing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until 1200 the office was held by a member of the Hashim clan, and descendants of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> kept it into the 20th century, ruling on behalf of Muslim powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. In 1517 the sharif acknowledged the supremacy of the Ottoman caliph but kept a large measure of local autonomy. During the Ottoman era the sharifate extended its rule north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, until then a sharifate of its own, and south to the frontiers of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, and it regularly raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>.</p><p>Hussein bin Ali, sharif from 1908, rebelled against Ottoman rule in the Arab Revolt of 1916. After the Ottoman defeat in 1918 he formed the independent Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and later declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, while the British gave his sons Faisal and Abdullah the new states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Transjordan. Facing growing attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, Hussein abdicated in 1924 in favour of his eldest son, Ali bin Hussein, the last Grand Sharif. Ibn Saud took the Hejaz at the end of 1925 and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Hashemites</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a> has overseen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> since then.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sharif of Mecca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruler was at first an emir, but Abu Zakariya’s son Muhammad I al-Mustansir (r. 1249–1277) took the caliphal title of commander of the faithful in 1253, and after the Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258 he was briefly recognised as caliph by the <b>Sharif of Mecca</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Faisal, son of the <b>Sharif of Mecca</b>, entered Damascus in October 1918 with the Arab army of the revolt against the Ottomans and set up a government under the British-led Occupied Enemy Territory Administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-kingdom-of-syria">Arab Kingdom of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
